WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.760
All right, so now
it's my pleasure

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.960
to welcome our speaker
for today, Albert Gu.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.680
He's an assistant professor in
the Machine Learning Department

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.920
at Carnegie Mellon University
and the chief scientist

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.640
of Cartesian AI.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.280
And his research
broadly focuses on

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.640
theoretical and empirical
foundations of deep learning.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.960
And he is particularly
known for new approaches

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.840
to deep sequence modeling and
neural network architectures.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.600
And was recognized
on the Time AI 100

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.300
list of most influential
researchers in 2024.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.480
And previously, he completed
his PhD here at Stanford.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.264
So, without further ado,
let's welcome Albert.

00:00:43.264 --> 00:00:46.480
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.880
All right, thank you, Steven.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:49.380
This work?

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.880
So I'll just get started.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:52.900
It's good to be back.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.140
I think I haven't been
back on campus in a while,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.660
even though it's only been
two years since I graduated.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.108
Just has a disclaimer,
so the talk

00:01:02.108 --> 00:01:04.150
is called On the Trade
Offs of State-Space Models

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.750
and Transformers.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.222
I've given versions of this
talk over the last year or so.

00:01:10.222 --> 00:01:11.930
And I turned it into
a blog post as well.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.930
So if you've seen those, this
talk won't be too different.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.590
There is a little
bit of new content,

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.550
but don't expect
anything that new.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.657
And afterwards, the
blog post will also

00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:24.490
be a good supplement
to this as well, if you

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.433
want to take a look at it.

00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:28.850
So in the last two
or three years,

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.710
there's been a big
surge of popularity

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.310
of alternative architectures
to transformers.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.030
Particularly models that
are so-called subquadratic

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.110
or linear in complexity.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:43.190
Some examples of these include
the original Mamba model,

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.830
which came out just
over two years ago

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:49.630
and arguably popularized a lot
of the subsequent line of work.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.710
There have now been
follow-ups to Mamba, Mamba 2,

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.430
and just a month ago,
we published Mamba 3.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:58.300
So there's continually
progress being made here.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.940
Other models you may
have heard include

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.060
xLSTM, which is a refinement
of the original LSTM, which

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.860
is in some ways the
original most popular RNN.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.620
That was the canonical sequence
model used before transformers.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.420
Other models that
are popular are

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.400
DeltaNet and the
follow-up Gated DeltaNet,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.540
which combines
DeltaNet with Mamba 2.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.980
This is another very
popular model that's been--

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.660
is now pretty widely used.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.160
And also another paradigm
called Test-Time Training,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.020
which views recurrence as
a form of test-time updates

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.075
that optimize an
optimization function.

00:02:34.075 --> 00:02:35.700
So these are a couple
umbrella families

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.820
of models that are
all quite popular now.

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.380
And we're seeing them
use more and more

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:46.340
in actual large-scale
production quality models.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:50.100
After Mamba came out, it became
used in a bunch of hybrid models

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.140
that combine these linear
layers with attention layers,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.050
including Jamba from
AI21, Zamba from Zyphra,

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.130
and Samba from Microsoft.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:01.970
More recently, there
are models that

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:07.410
include Hunyuan from
Tencent, which was actually

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.970
several hundred
billion parameters.

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:14.690
The latest Qwen models are
now based on Gated DeltaNet.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.170
Kimi-Linear is
another hybrid model

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:21.970
that's based on some
follow-up data DeltaNet.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.810
I think very recently, AI2
also released a new Olmo model

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.170
that is hybrid, and it
also uses Gated DeltaNet

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:30.970
combined with attention.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:33.610
And somewhere here I forgot
to list the Nemotron models

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.550
from NVIDIA are also very new.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:40.010
I think the latest one is
the Nemontron 3 models, which

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.010
have been scaled to,
again, hundreds of billions

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.090
of parameters.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.950
And that model uses Mamba 2.

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.250
So again, these models are
actually very, very widely used

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.090
now.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.490
And so today, I'm
going to be talking

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.070
about a lot of these models.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.500
And rather than focusing
on any particular one,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.200
I'm going to zoom out and talk
about general characteristics

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.663
of these models, how do
I think about them, how

00:04:02.663 --> 00:04:05.080
do I think about transformers,
and how these models relate

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:05.820
to each other.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.360
There's a lot of
different models

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.000
here, like these
and many, many more.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.260
And there's a lot of names
used to refer to them.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.079
So in this talk, I'm
going to call them

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:24.240
state-space models, which is
what Mamba was considered to be.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.880
But there are actually many
different lineages of work

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.280
that have appeared in parallel.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.520
And there are other
names for related models

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.040
that, for the most
part, at least

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.700
for the purposes of this talk,
are largely interchangeable.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.400
So other things that
you can call these

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.040
include linear attention.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.740
They're all kind of
recurrent models.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.560
So you can call them
just recurrent models.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.480
Sometimes called modern
recurrent models,

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.840
as opposed to the older
ones like the LSTM.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.750
You can call them linear RNNs.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.510
Or just broadly, linear models.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.330
So usually these days when
people say any of these words,

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:05.670
it kind of broadly refers to
really any of these models.

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.270
And when we say
hybrid models, we

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.910
mean one of these combined
with quadratic attention.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.370
So these, again, are actually
generic architectures.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.232
And they can be used
pretty much anywhere.

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:17.690
But for the most
part in this talk,

00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:20.230
we will also focus on
autoregressive modeling.

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:24.470
So this is something you're
probably all familiar with.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.430
Paradigm, such as
for language models

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.070
where you have a model that
models next token probabilities

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.250
of a sequence.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.252
And then it can act
as a generative model

00:05:35.252 --> 00:05:36.710
where you can sample
from the model

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:39.270
by repeatedly sampling a
token and feeding it back

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:41.390
to the model.

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:44.250
The reason we focus on
this is for two reasons.

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.470
First of all, it's, in some
sense, the most important

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.095
modeling paradigm
right now, especially

00:05:48.095 --> 00:05:49.310
for language modeling.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:52.030
And second of all, for
understanding the trade offs

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.400
of these different
sequence models,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.540
it's actually helpful to think
about autoregressive modeling.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:01.860
And so just to put us
all on the same page,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.420
we'll really quickly go over
what does inference or sampling

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.380
look like for these models.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:11.100
So I'm not going to redefine
attention everything.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.920
But from a zoomed-out
point of view,

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.780
what attention does
at inference time

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:21.180
is that you give it a prompt,
which is a set of tokens,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:27.860
and then the model tries to
predict the next token or word

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.700
by doing these
pairwise comparisons

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.840
against every single previous
token that it's seen.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.080
So you do these comparisons,
you run some calculations,

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.982
and then you can
predict the next word.

00:06:39.982 --> 00:06:41.940
And then you can put this
back into your prompt

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:42.815
and then repeat this.

00:06:42.815 --> 00:06:45.860
So what characterizes
attention is this ability

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.260
to look over every
single past token

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.370
that you've seen in the context.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:56.250
So even without defining
exactly what attention is,

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.210
this, I think, is a very
useful characterization of it.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:02.610
Because I think of transformers
as the canonical model

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.490
that caches every past token.

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:06.450
Because it needs to cache
every token in order

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:09.613
to do these comparisons.

00:07:09.613 --> 00:07:11.030
To get a little
bit more specific,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.530
this is called the
KV cache, which I

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.010
think everyone probably knows.

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:17.130
A lot of the work
around transformers

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:20.130
basically has to deal with the
KV cache, ways of compressing

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.450
it and so on and so forth.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:25.090
And again, it doesn't
actually matter

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.510
that it's called the KV cache.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:28.802
That's an artifact of the
way transformers are defined.

00:07:28.802 --> 00:07:31.330
But at an even higher level,
basically, transformer

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.575
is just a model that
stores this cache.

00:07:33.575 --> 00:07:34.950
And because it
stores this cache,

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.970
this really defines its
computational characteristics.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.575
In particular, it's heavily
dependent on the size

00:07:40.575 --> 00:07:42.450
of its context and the
memory and computation

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:45.650
scales with the context.

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:50.640
Now if you amortize this
over inference, then instead

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.260
of scaling linearly
with the context,

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.220
the model scales
quadratically in compute.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:59.000
And so this is why attention
is considered quadratic model.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:01.960
And at a high level,
again, what attention does

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.240
is it does all
these comparisons.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:05.800
And what [INAUDIBLE]
models do is

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:11.800
that, again, in inference time,
as your token stream in, instead

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:15.520
of storing all of them
explicitly, the model

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.760
squishes them all into
a state, basically,

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.520
represented by this
blue ball here.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.920
And then the individual tokens
are kind of thrown away,

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:26.833
and the model only interacts
with data through the state.

00:08:26.833 --> 00:08:28.500
And then it does this
repeatedly online.

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.159
So you can generate new token
and put it back into your state.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.836
The state is highly
compressed, which

00:08:33.836 --> 00:08:36.419
means that every single step of
inference takes constant time.

00:08:36.419 --> 00:08:39.020
And then summing this over a
sequence takes linear time.

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:41.520
So this is why we call
them linear models.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.590
And so this is really
just a high-level way

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:44.340
to think about it.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:46.400
And these are the ways
that really distinguish

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.230
these families of models.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.670
So now let me talk a little
bit about the actual definition

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:54.790
of these models and what I
think are the most important

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:56.270
ingredients that go in.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:59.190
So first of all,
state-space models

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:03.070
are basically defined by
this equation down here.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:08.230
So it is essentially a
special type of recurrence

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.230
with a few key characteristics.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:14.470
We think of this recurrence as a
sequence mapping from an input,

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:17.965
x, to an output, y.

00:09:17.965 --> 00:09:20.090
And when you unbind this
along the sequence length,

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.423
you can think of x as basically
a one-dimensional number

00:09:22.423 --> 00:09:24.830
or a scalar.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:28.630
It gets blown up
with a vector B,

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:30.830
and then summed into
some transformation

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:31.610
of a hidden state.

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:33.745
So every step, just like
a normal occurrence,

00:09:33.745 --> 00:09:36.370
you update the hidden state and
then you incorporate the input.

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:38.830
And then there's this
recurrent hidden state

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:43.790
denoted by h that kind
characterizes recurrent models.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:48.100
Some differences between SSMs
and some of the older RNNs

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:53.660
are that first of all,
this recurrence is linear.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:57.620
There's no non-linearity that's
applied after this equation

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:02.260
like they are in GRUs, LSTMs
and some of the older RNNs.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.900
Second of all, some
of the big differences

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:06.980
include these ingredients.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:09.180
So the first kind
of main one that

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.820
differs a lot from
past traditional RNNs

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:16.300
is that the state
size is much larger.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:18.540
So the way I define
this is that x here

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.045
is a one-dimensional input.

00:10:21.045 --> 00:10:23.420
And for a one-dimensional
input, the hidden state becomes

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.020
N-dimensional, where N is
usually on the order of--

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:28.600
it's like 64 or 128 or so.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:32.420
So the state is 100 times
bigger than the input.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.940
And so this is very different
than how LSTMs were defined.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.100
The reason this is
important is because

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:43.620
your autoregressive state here
is a bottleneck of your context,

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.230
because all the context got
compressed into the state.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:49.090
So the larger your state is, the
more information your model can

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:51.290
remember, which is really
important on information

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:54.930
dense modalities, such
as for language modeling.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:57.850
The second key ingredient is
that not only does the state

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:00.410
have to be large, but it
has to be expressive enough

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:03.570
to remember exactly what
the model wants to remember.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:05.570
So abstractly, you
can think of any RNN

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.610
as a function that combines
the previous hidden state

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.410
with a new input.

00:11:10.410 --> 00:11:12.170
And so you have a
black box function,

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:14.128
and different parameterizations
of the function

00:11:14.128 --> 00:11:15.410
define different RNNs.

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.690
And making this more
expressive allows the model

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:23.970
to be more precise about the
information it's modeling.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:29.670
So one key difference with
some of the later SSMs,

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:32.650
such as Mamba compared
to older models,

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.770
is that the parameters
here are defined to be

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.170
functions of the input itself.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.810
And this is, again, different
not only from older SSMs

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.902
but also, again,
different from LSTMs.

00:11:44.902 --> 00:11:46.360
We call this
selectivity because it

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:49.600
means that the model, by having
its own parameters be functions

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:53.040
of the input, the
model can control,

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:56.560
based on the input, how to
control the recurrence, which

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:58.880
lets the model be more
precise in selecting

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:00.600
what information to remember.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:04.800
Because for example, if A here
is a function of the input

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:06.720
and I don't want to
remember the input,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:10.960
then I can look at the
input and then decide

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.240
that I want A to be 0.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.040
Or sorry, I want A to
be 1 and B to be 0.

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:17.860
Or if I really want
to remember the input,

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:21.160
maybe throw away the previous
context, I could set A to be 0.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:24.000
So this selectivity makes the
state update function much more

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.700
expressive.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:26.200
And this was really
a key ingredient

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:28.760
in getting these
models to be effective.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:32.380
The final ingredient is that
both the first two ingredients,

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.360
the state size and the
more expressive state

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.400
update function, make the
model much more expressive

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.220
and have much more capacity.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.070
But also makes the model
much harder to compute.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:47.230
And so in particular,
unlike a traditional RNN,

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:48.790
it's actually
basically impossible

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:51.150
to compute this by
computing the recurrence.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:52.670
And so a lot of
the work involved

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:58.630
in these family of models was
to find clever ways of rewriting

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.690
the computation, to compute the
training parts of this much,

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:02.450
much faster.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:07.610
Some ideas here include
associative scan,

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:09.950
which is what the
original Mamba model used.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:12.930
Basically by exploiting the fact
that this recurrence is linear,

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:14.550
you can parallelize
the computation

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.510
using a specific algorithm.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:18.590
And later versions of
this, including Mamba 2

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:21.030
and including many
of the other variants

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.990
like the Gated
Delta-Net, completely

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:29.310
rewrite this in terms of this
chunked matrix multiplication.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.125
So really, I think
these are the three

00:13:32.125 --> 00:13:33.250
most important ingredients.

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.630
There's a lot more
details that are involved

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:36.590
in how exactly do
parametrize all of these,

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:39.990
how to initialize all
of these, and so on.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:43.260
But I think these are
the main reasons that

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:45.060
made these models effective.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.180
And all of these
ingredients actually

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:50.400
existed prior to this
recent line of work.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.080
For example, linear attention
first was published, I think,

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:55.733
in 2020.

00:13:55.733 --> 00:13:57.900
And it basically used
something similar to this that

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:00.780
had this state size expansion.

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.660
Classical RNNs can be viewed
as some form of selectivity

00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:05.980
or gating.

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.980
And many models have
already used these ideas

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:12.180
for more efficient computation,
including these older

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.560
linear RNNs.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:17.900
All these ingredients
were used before,

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:20.740
but Mamba can be viewed as
the first model that actually

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:22.040
combined all three of these.

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:25.620
And that was critical for it to
be actually really effective.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.453
And it was perhaps
the first model

00:14:27.453 --> 00:14:29.620
that demonstrated that
recurrent models can actually

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:34.300
be competitive with transformers
on information-dense modalities

00:14:34.300 --> 00:14:36.620
like language.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:39.930
We now that this statement
has a bit of a caveat.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.310
There is much more nuance to
modeling than just perplexity,

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:44.110
for example.

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:46.890
But this certainly
showed that from

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:49.198
a coarse-grained
perspective like perplexity,

00:14:49.198 --> 00:14:51.490
these completely different
architectures could actually

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.530
be competitive.

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.890
Since then, there's been,
again, a lot of work.

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:59.210
This is a nice table
from the DeltaNet paper.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:01.530
But since then, there's
been even more models.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.697
But basically, there's a
lot of different variations

00:15:03.697 --> 00:15:06.190
of these models that
are really effective.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:09.230
But I think all of them are kind
of pretty similar to each other.

00:15:09.230 --> 00:15:12.610
They're all variants that all
include these three ingredients

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:16.470
and tweak different
definitions of them.

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:18.748
So many of these vary the
state update a little bit,

00:15:18.748 --> 00:15:20.790
some of them change the
computation a little bit,

00:15:20.790 --> 00:15:24.050
but all care a lot
about efficiency.

00:15:24.050 --> 00:15:27.970
And to give a
off-the-record kind

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:32.210
of what I think are the most
effective ones, probably right

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:35.610
now, the most tried and
true variants of these

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.593
are Mamba 2 and Gated DeltaNet.

00:15:37.593 --> 00:15:39.760
Those are also the ones
that are most popularly used

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:42.120
in large-scale hybrid models.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:46.280
Gated DeltaNet is a little more
powerful, but a little slower

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:47.180
version of Mamba 2.

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:49.940
So depending on your needs,
both of these are pretty good,

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.000
but it depends on
what exactly you want.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.820
Mamba 3 just came out and it's a
little bit less tested at scale,

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:57.120
but also will
probably be up there.

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:59.247
But more importantly, all
of these models I think

00:15:59.247 --> 00:16:01.080
are actually much more
similar to each other

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.240
than they are to attention.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.920
Instead of talking about the
exact details between all

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:09.080
of these, I think it's
more interesting to think

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:11.038
about the higher level
trade offs between these

00:16:11.038 --> 00:16:12.680
and attention.

00:16:12.680 --> 00:16:16.120
So that's what the rest
of the talk will be about.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.400
So again, we're
going to examine this

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:20.460
through the nature of
autoregressive modeling.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:23.120
And one of the
key points here is

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.320
that I claim that the trade
offs of sequence models

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.120
can be understood
through examining

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:29.960
their autoregressive state.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.040
What is the
autoregressive state?

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:34.640
So in some sense, every
autoregressive model

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:37.250
can be viewed as carrying
an implicit state.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:42.990
So this can literally
be defined as if you're

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:45.830
examining an autoregressive
model during generation time,

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.310
like in this
animation, the state

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:51.190
is what the model stores in
memory in between each step

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:54.030
of generation.

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:56.830
So for an SSM, the
state is just going

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.910
to be its fixed size, kind
of a matrix-valued state.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:05.150
For attention, the state is
going to be its KV cache.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:07.150
And the definitions
of these states

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:11.150
really inform the trade
offs of these models

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.608
and their inductive biases.

00:17:14.608 --> 00:17:16.650
Again, we talked about
how these models generate.

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:21.270
So transformers are
quadratic by looking

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:22.869
at every single past token.

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:25.550
And SSMs are linear time
because they compress everything

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:27.790
into a state.

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:31.670
A simple analogy that I like
is that I think of transformers

00:17:31.670 --> 00:17:32.910
like a database.

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.620
So if you think about the
nature of its state, which

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.500
is the KV cache,
what it's doing is

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.940
it's storing a representation
of every single token

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.940
or every single element of a
sequence that's seen before.

00:17:42.940 --> 00:17:45.120
And it's basically writing
it down to its database.

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:46.520
And as the sequence
length grows,

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:48.660
it's kind of expanding
its database.

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:52.180
And it's able to attend or
look back very precisely

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.940
onto any element
of this database.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:59.460
On the other hand, SSMs are
more like a brain in that it

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.900
is a fixed-size method.

00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.400
And again, it kind of
compresses all the information

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:08.620
it's seen into this
fixed-size state.

00:18:08.620 --> 00:18:13.060
So this has a completely
different characteristics

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:14.860
than a database.

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:17.180
And many of the trade
offs of these models

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.940
can be kind of intuited
from this analogy.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:24.740
So for example, some of
the weaknesses of SSMs

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.580
are that they are not
so good at retrieval.

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:30.420
And this is exactly the way
that brains work as well.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.940
Humans are notoriously bad
at remembering exact strings

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:36.210
of numbers and so on.

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.030
But it also comes
with advantages.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:46.050
So at a first pass, so talking
about the trade offs of SSMs,

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:50.370
they're the canonical,
stateful and compressive model.

00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.250
Statefulness is really
powerful because it's

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:55.090
what allows these
models to be really

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:58.048
efficient in online settings.

00:18:58.048 --> 00:18:59.590
Kind of, again, just
like brains are,

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:01.890
it's always online, always
able to consume information

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.930
at a constant rate and interact
with the world in real time.

00:19:05.930 --> 00:19:08.410
These are places that
these linear models

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:11.450
are likely to be very good at.

00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:15.570
The compressiveness of it also
has subtle benefits, which I'll

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.170
talk about later in the talk.

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:19.670
And the downsides are that,
because of the fixed-size state,

00:19:19.670 --> 00:19:22.530
these lack fine-grained recall
and retrieval abilities.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:26.130
So there's many kind of
synthetic tasks like Haystack,

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:30.250
associative recall, these things
that involve asking a query

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:34.950
and digging out a precise bit of
information from your context.

00:19:34.950 --> 00:19:39.750
These models are not so good
at it, just like brains are.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:41.950
Now again, the analogy
has more implications.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:45.410
Because for example, if you
think about human intelligence,

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:47.150
it's actually not
just the brain.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:50.850
Humans use a lot of
external tools to assist,

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:54.290
and that this really augments
our ability to process,

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:58.070
and it augments intelligence.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:00.330
So if you think of
human-like intelligence

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:04.750
really as the combination
of our processing unit

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:10.230
as well as external
scratchpads, then this analogy

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:12.450
also predicts the
behavior of models.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:17.390
And so this is one high-level
inspiration for hybrid models.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:20.450
Starting from the advent of
these state-space models,

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:24.430
they've been used in hybrid
models for this reason.

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.230
There's a few references
here that refer

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:28.790
to some of the older ones.

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:33.780
But again, there's now many,
many more versions some of which

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:36.220
are very, very large scale.

00:20:36.220 --> 00:20:38.700
And one thing that's
been pretty remarkable

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:43.100
is that many different
groups independently

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:47.357
verified the optimal
ratios of hybrid models.

00:20:47.357 --> 00:20:48.940
So the simplest way
to define a hybrid

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:52.540
is just to interleave
your linear layer

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:54.100
with your quadratic
attention layer.

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:56.820
And then the question is,
what ratio of these layers

00:20:56.820 --> 00:20:59.100
should you include?

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:02.780
Many of these older papers found
something remarkably consistent

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.140
that it's something close to
a 10-to-1 ratio of SSM layers

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.140
to attention layers
seem optimal,

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:11.380
at least from a
perplexity standpoint.

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:13.860
Nowadays, this
ratio seems to have

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:17.420
changed a little bit as models
have been getting better.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:21.300
But I think that it's still very
consistent that people typically

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:24.660
use something like, at minimum,
a 3-to-1 or 4-to-1 ratio

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:27.740
of linear layers to
quadratic layers.

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:29.330
And so what's
interesting about this

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:31.170
is that if you use
the analogy and you

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:34.630
think about human intelligence,
you might also predict this.

00:21:34.630 --> 00:21:38.490
Because we tend to think of the
brain as the main processing

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:42.670
unit and the external databases,
scratchpads and so on,

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:46.470
as a supplement that helps
you store information,

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:49.170
but is not actually the
main computational lifter.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:52.290
And so the fact that when you
actually do these very careful

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:54.930
ablations and find that you
want more linear layers than

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:57.590
quadratic-- and this is not
accounting for computation,

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.437
it's just what makes
the best performance--

00:21:59.437 --> 00:22:01.770
it's pretty interesting that
kind of follows the analogy

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:03.490
as well.

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:09.490
And so a thought is that,
while these linear models are

00:22:09.490 --> 00:22:11.530
sometimes viewed as
having a critical drawback

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:14.510
because the finite state means
you can't remember everything,

00:22:14.510 --> 00:22:18.130
it seems like this
compression is a weakness,

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:19.368
it's actually not so clear.

00:22:19.368 --> 00:22:21.410
Because even if we're just
looking at performance

00:22:21.410 --> 00:22:24.770
without looking at
speed, you actually do

00:22:24.770 --> 00:22:26.550
want a lot of
linear layers here.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:30.052
And so I think a
general theme is about,

00:22:30.052 --> 00:22:31.760
is the compression
actually fundamentally

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.880
important to intelligence, and
what role does it play there?

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:38.120
And once again, there will be
a very interesting experiment

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:41.440
I'll show later that hopefully
touches on this again.

00:22:41.440 --> 00:22:46.840
So now we're going to switch and
talk about just transformers.

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:49.600
So although there's
a lot of work

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.440
here on transformer
alternatives,

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:54.920
transformers are
still the to-go model

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:56.540
for many things,
especially language.

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:57.420
And for good reason.

00:22:57.420 --> 00:23:00.080
They're very, very powerful.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:04.520
And there's a prevailing
mindset that they basically

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:05.700
work on everything.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:09.240
And they're really used
in pretty much all fields,

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.520
from language to
vision to many more.

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:15.080
And many people think that
you basically should just

00:23:15.080 --> 00:23:18.820
use a transformer and just
put your data into it,

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:21.120
and it just works.

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:22.840
And this is true, actually.

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:24.480
But there are nuances to it.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:28.310
So the way I think about it
is that attention is actually

00:23:28.310 --> 00:23:31.510
most effective when your
data is at the right level

00:23:31.510 --> 00:23:33.590
of abstraction.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:35.530
What this means is
that in any pipeline

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:38.670
you actually see where
transformers are used,

00:23:38.670 --> 00:23:41.710
there are pretty substantial
encoder decoder layers

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:44.150
that transform the
data to a form that

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:46.590
is suitable for a
transformative process.

00:23:46.590 --> 00:23:48.470
So to give some examples.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:52.870
When you use these models
in vision domains--

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:56.270
for example, this
figure is from I

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:57.990
think the original
vision transformer

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:01.270
paper for classification--

00:24:01.270 --> 00:24:02.950
what they do is
they take your image

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:06.150
and divide it into these
patches, which are basically

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:07.870
vision tokens.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.230
And then encode it a
little bit and then pass it

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:12.430
into the transformer.

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:16.070
And so basically, it's kind of
making your data much coarser

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:20.750
and in a form that's more
suitable for the transformer.

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:23.030
And I claim that this
encoding step is actually

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:25.980
critical for the
transformer to work well.

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:29.180
In language, this encoder
is called the tokenizer.

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:33.500
So if you have a
string of characters,

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.780
like if you're modeling
English, technically, you

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:40.180
could just put this into your
model and it should work.

00:24:40.180 --> 00:24:43.540
But we actually process it using
a tokenization step that splits

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:47.140
it into these coarser chunks.

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:49.880
And many people actually don't
really think about this step.

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:52.745
We kind of always just use
off-the-shelf tokenization.

00:24:52.745 --> 00:24:54.620
But again, I claim that
this step is actually

00:24:54.620 --> 00:24:56.640
really critical for
transformers to work.

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.540
Not just for efficiency
reasons, but actually

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:00.740
from a modeling
perspective in terms

00:25:00.740 --> 00:25:04.820
of what features and
transformations the transformer

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:07.300
can capture.

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.100
So tokenization is low
notorious because it

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:11.100
has a lot of problems.

00:25:11.100 --> 00:25:15.180
So we can ask what happens
if you don't tokenize.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:18.360
There's a number of outspoken
critics against tokenization.

00:25:18.360 --> 00:25:22.020
This is a tweet taken
from Andrej Karpathy where

00:25:22.020 --> 00:25:27.450
he kind of enumerates a bunch
of edge cases with tokenization.

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:34.250
It causes issues in spelling,
It has many edge cases.

00:25:34.250 --> 00:25:38.090
If you ever kind of prompt
your model with out ending it

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:43.730
in a space, or maybe it's ending
it in a space, one of these two

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:44.870
will screw up your model.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:46.287
So you have to be
very deliberate.

00:25:46.287 --> 00:25:49.130
And these are all edge
cases and tokenization.

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:52.445
However, most of these
edge cases are understood.

00:25:52.445 --> 00:25:54.570
You can engineer away a
lot of these with good data

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.250
or with other tricks.

00:25:56.250 --> 00:25:59.330
And so many people think that
this is not a real problem.

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:01.250
But if you could
solve tokenization,

00:26:01.250 --> 00:26:04.890
or if you can get rid of it,
you would solve these for free.

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:10.310
And I think that even if the
engineering solution just works,

00:26:10.310 --> 00:26:12.570
it's still a worthwhile
problem to address

00:26:12.570 --> 00:26:14.330
for philosophical reasons.

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:19.810
Because AI has always been
about learning automatically

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:20.970
from raw data.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:23.240
And models that are
more and more end-to-end

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:25.240
generally tend to be better.

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:28.000
This also reflects
on the bitter lesson.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:30.160
If you can find models
that are more generic

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:32.800
and work with less
feature engineering,

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:34.308
but benefit more
from scale, you'll

00:26:34.308 --> 00:26:36.600
eventually get better models
that learn better features

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.567
from data.

00:26:38.567 --> 00:26:40.400
So what happens if you
actually train models

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:41.560
without tokenization?

00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:43.280
So one simple
thing you can do is

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:46.817
just look at language
modeling but not

00:26:46.817 --> 00:26:47.900
tokenize your data before.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:50.640
So basically, you can just
pass in raw characters

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.480
into your model.

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:58.422
So this is a plot from
a recent paper of ours.

00:26:58.422 --> 00:27:00.880
But it was first done by this
paper called MambaByte, which

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:04.400
basically just tried to compare
Mamba against transformers

00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.400
on byte-level modeling.

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:08.480
So again, here,
you basically just

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:11.200
don't use your BPE
tokenizer on the data.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:14.560
And these are some
training curves

00:27:14.560 --> 00:27:17.520
for models that
are matched in size

00:27:17.520 --> 00:27:20.910
and just trained for longer.

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:27.070
So what you see here is that the
SSM here is actually much, much

00:27:27.070 --> 00:27:28.370
better than the transformer.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:32.910
So the purple line here is a
sliding-window attention model.

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:36.170
And the blue line is
just the Mamba model.

00:27:36.170 --> 00:27:39.350
And these are isotropic
models that are basically

00:27:39.350 --> 00:27:41.290
equivalent in every way.

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:44.590
And you can see there's a
very, very large gap here.

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:47.870
If you draw the horizontal
line to see the data efficiency

00:27:47.870 --> 00:27:51.670
difference, it's
basically 2x or something.

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:54.510
And even if you let the
attention be global,

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:57.010
and in this case because
byte sequences are so long,

00:27:57.010 --> 00:28:01.070
this is really increasing the
computation so that the global

00:28:01.070 --> 00:28:05.110
attention model in the dashed
line here is doing 2x as much

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:10.350
compute as the Mamba model,
it's actually still not as good.

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:12.990
And so what this means
is that there's actually

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:14.430
a fundamental difference here.

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:19.580
And it's not just because
of that attention is slower.

00:28:19.580 --> 00:28:21.780
The comparison between
these two bottom lines

00:28:21.780 --> 00:28:26.540
are the same model size, same
data, same sequence length,

00:28:26.540 --> 00:28:29.340
and it's simply letting the
attention do its quadratic thing

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:31.020
and using much more compute.

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:33.380
But it's still a bit worse.

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:37.560
So this phenomenon occurs
in other settings too.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.660
So we can look at sequence-like
data that is not just

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:46.503
language or language with
alternative vocabularies.

00:28:46.503 --> 00:28:48.420
This is a figure from
the original Mamba paper

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:52.220
that we investigated the
model on DNA modeling.

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:54.540
So again, you can basically
just take DNA sequences

00:28:54.540 --> 00:28:57.820
and do autoregressive
modeling on them.

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:01.740
And here, the comparison between
the orange and the red line

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:06.300
is the Mamba versus transformer,
where, once again, we

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.400
see that there's actually
a really big gap.

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:12.180
And if you try to match for
compute or match for parameters,

00:29:12.180 --> 00:29:16.900
the Mamba model is
about 3x more efficient.

00:29:16.900 --> 00:29:19.930
So these are settings
that basically

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:21.730
don't have tokenization.

00:29:21.730 --> 00:29:24.330
And so what is going on here?

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:26.953
So when I think about
what attention is good at,

00:29:26.953 --> 00:29:28.370
there's a couple
heuristics that I

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:34.490
think are really helpful for
thinking about what it does

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.348
and the types of settings
that it excels at.

00:29:37.348 --> 00:29:38.890
So first of all,
once again, thinking

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:41.810
about the autoregressive state.

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:44.850
So we thought of the attention
as storing a database

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:47.130
of every token it's seen.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:52.210
That means that it should
make sense to actually cache

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:53.450
every single token.

00:29:55.992 --> 00:29:57.450
You can think of
many types of data

00:29:57.450 --> 00:30:00.610
where it actually doesn't make
sense to cache every token.

00:30:00.610 --> 00:30:03.610
Obvious examples include
if your data is noisy

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:07.130
and there's spurious
tokens that you don't need.

00:30:07.130 --> 00:30:13.890
Or you can think, perhaps,
in the language setting.

00:30:13.890 --> 00:30:15.760
When we read
English, we can often

00:30:15.760 --> 00:30:19.060
identify words, even if you
drop out a lot of the tokens.

00:30:19.060 --> 00:30:22.600
And that's just a
very coarse analogy

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:25.920
that shows that you don't need
to spend all of that memory, all

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.420
that computation, to cache a
representation for every token,

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:31.600
because not every
token is meaningful.

00:30:31.600 --> 00:30:35.600
Another heuristic is
that, in some ways,

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:38.400
softmax attention was
developed as a alternative

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:39.780
to hard attention.

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:42.025
What hard attention
means is that--

00:30:42.025 --> 00:30:43.400
softmax attention,
basically, you

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:46.480
take a weighted combination of
every token in your context.

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:48.280
Hard attention
means that imagine

00:30:48.280 --> 00:30:53.400
you can only look at a
single token at a time.

00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:55.480
So this is also, I
think, a useful proxy

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:57.040
for what attention does.

00:30:57.040 --> 00:31:01.520
And once again, it's
helpful to think about this

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:05.920
because attention
excels at data where

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:07.920
hard attention makes sense.

00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:11.360
And so this is why, again,
use applying attention to,

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:15.530
say, character level data
is much less effective,

00:31:15.530 --> 00:31:19.070
because if you can think
about the nature of the data

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:21.230
and how we process
it, you're never going

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:23.590
to look at a single character.

00:31:23.590 --> 00:31:25.630
You're kind of paying
attention to the data

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:29.150
at the semantic level of
words or even coarser,

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:31.150
not at the level of characters.

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:35.430
And this kind of is a
very heuristic explanation

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:38.390
for the phenomenon
that we just saw.

00:31:38.390 --> 00:31:41.510
And so if we look at the
different types of data

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:44.550
that you can use
this really, I think

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:48.150
is useful for thinking about
where attention is useful.

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:51.590
So if you have things like
words or subwords, which

00:31:51.590 --> 00:31:54.790
is what we typically
apply transformers on,

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:58.510
they really excel because
these tokens are intrinsically

00:31:58.510 --> 00:32:00.150
semantically meaningful.

00:32:00.150 --> 00:32:01.490
They're modular and composable.

00:32:01.490 --> 00:32:04.630
It makes sense to want
to look at a single word

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:07.310
or store a
representation of a word.

00:32:07.310 --> 00:32:10.390
When we start looking at other
settings, they make less sense.

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:14.940
If we're modeling characters
or DNA base pairs,

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:16.740
every individual
character basically

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:20.700
doesn't have any meaning,
every individual DNA base

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:21.800
doesn't have any meaning.

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:24.900
And so this is one explanation
for why attention does so much

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.100
worse here than other models.

00:32:28.100 --> 00:32:31.420
And many other settings
are not so clean.

00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:34.980
For example, in vision, if you
look at a patch of an image,

00:32:34.980 --> 00:32:36.960
it's not really clear
which camp you fall into.

00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:40.423
Some of the patches may
have a lot of information.

00:32:40.423 --> 00:32:41.840
Some of them may
have very little.

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:43.580
You might just get a
patch of sky that's

00:32:43.580 --> 00:32:45.000
kind of useless to your model.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:49.020
And so the trade offs there
are a little bit less clear.

00:32:49.020 --> 00:32:51.660
But it goes to show
why when we move away

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:55.100
from pure tokenized
language, actually,

00:32:55.100 --> 00:32:59.700
these alternative models
become more and more popular.

00:32:59.700 --> 00:33:03.260
And I think it's because of
basically this reason that when

00:33:03.260 --> 00:33:07.220
you don't have very
well defined tokenizers,

00:33:07.220 --> 00:33:09.020
the power of
transformers goes down

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:12.350
and the power of models that are
doing this implicit compression,

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:14.290
which in some ways
can be viewed maybe

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:16.690
as sharing a similar
role as tokenization,

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:18.130
these models get better.

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:21.530
So this is a graph taken pretty
soon after the Mamba paper came

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:28.210
out about where it was most
popularly used in applications.

00:33:28.210 --> 00:33:33.450
And even though the model was
kind of pitched as a language

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:37.570
model, actually language
only took up about 10%

00:33:37.570 --> 00:33:38.730
of the applications.

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:41.850
And instead, it was viewed as
just a generally useful sequence

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:43.610
model for all sorts
of modalities,

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:47.750
from time series to audio
to especially vision.

00:33:50.450 --> 00:33:55.050
So now, while we're on
the topic of tokenizers,

00:33:55.050 --> 00:33:57.670
a very recent application
of these models has been,

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:01.410
again, in the domain of
tokenizer-free models.

00:34:01.410 --> 00:34:05.410
So very recently,
my group published

00:34:05.410 --> 00:34:10.360
a paper called H-Net, which
is maybe the latest model that

00:34:10.360 --> 00:34:13.239
tries to do actual
tokenizer-free modeling

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:15.639
of language.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:19.960
So in a nutshell, the way I
would describe H-Net is that

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:21.960
it's an end-to-end
hierarchical network--

00:34:21.960 --> 00:34:26.320
and that's what it stands
for, hierarchical network--

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:29.159
that operates on raw
data and compresses it

00:34:29.159 --> 00:34:32.880
through a data-dependent
chunking process.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:35.120
So I'll unpack this
in a little bit.

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:37.600
But basically you think
of this as a model that

00:34:37.600 --> 00:34:41.199
is operating on raw
data and implicitly

00:34:41.199 --> 00:34:46.000
trying to tokenize it by
chunking it inside the model.

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:50.199
This work was largely led by my
student [INAUDIBLE] Hwang here.

00:34:50.199 --> 00:34:53.280
And this is the
name of the paper.

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:56.320
This is a really general
neural network architecture

00:34:56.320 --> 00:35:00.400
that's really about the
idea of compression.

00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:04.360
Where it's most obviously
useful is simply

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:06.760
on tokenizer-free
language modeling.

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:11.830
So to contrast, again, the way
that all of our language models

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.790
work is through an
explicit tokenization step.

00:35:14.790 --> 00:35:18.390
If you're familiar with
this, the most common one

00:35:18.390 --> 00:35:20.550
is called BPE or
byte-pair encoding.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.030
And it's basically
some form of heuristics

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:26.750
that take your sentence
of low-level data,

00:35:26.750 --> 00:35:32.470
like characters, and then
implement some merge rules

00:35:32.470 --> 00:35:34.490
that group them together
into bigger chunks.

00:35:34.490 --> 00:35:37.270
So for example, this sentence
might be tokenized exactly

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:42.190
like this where characters got
merged together into these--

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:45.950
I'm calling them chunks, but
we usually call them tokens.

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:48.390
What the H-Net does
is that instead

00:35:48.390 --> 00:35:50.690
of doing this as a
separate offline step

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:53.030
and then passing
these into the model,

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:57.670
the H-Net only sees the
original characters.

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:00.270
So, for example, the
vocab size would be just,

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:03.350
say, like 256 if you're
working on bytes.

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:07.050
And then the model, it's looking
for boundaries like this.

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:09.420
But this is all happening
inside the model.

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:12.580
And this is a visualization
of what the model actually

00:36:12.580 --> 00:36:14.700
might do during training.

00:36:14.700 --> 00:36:19.500
So this depicts the
training stage of the model.

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:22.620
I think the animation started
partway through for some reason.

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:25.820
But basically, the model
is modeling just bytes.

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:27.660
And the green denotes
where the model

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:30.127
is trying to put boundaries.

00:36:30.127 --> 00:36:31.960
And so this is at the
beginning of training,

00:36:31.960 --> 00:36:34.100
the model is doing
a lot of exploring

00:36:34.100 --> 00:36:35.860
because it doesn't how.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.887
And then it eventually
kind of stabilizes

00:36:37.887 --> 00:36:40.220
on boundaries that are quite
aligned with where we would

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.220
expect a good tokenizer to do.

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:44.780
In particular on
English, it happens

00:36:44.780 --> 00:36:47.500
to coincide that
your tokens generally

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:49.060
want to be where spaces are.

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:53.600
But if you look closely, you can
see that the model is like here,

00:36:53.600 --> 00:36:54.355
for example--

00:36:54.355 --> 00:36:55.480
OK, the animations started.

00:36:55.480 --> 00:36:56.660
But you can see that
the model is actually

00:36:56.660 --> 00:36:59.020
finding semantically
meaningful subwords as well.

00:36:59.020 --> 00:37:00.632
And if you compose
this, you'll find

00:37:00.632 --> 00:37:02.340
that the model finds
semantically meaning

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:05.220
groups of words.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:08.890
So this was a major step
towards actually doing

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:13.450
tokenizer-free modeling without
the separate offline step.

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:16.163
So I won't go fully
into the details

00:37:16.163 --> 00:37:17.830
because the model is
pretty complicated,

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:22.170
but I'll try to give a high
level of how this works.

00:37:22.170 --> 00:37:27.010
So this, here on the left,
depicts the general structure

00:37:27.010 --> 00:37:29.068
of the model.

00:37:29.068 --> 00:37:30.610
And so we think of
it as hierarchical

00:37:30.610 --> 00:37:35.422
because there are
two main stages.

00:37:35.422 --> 00:37:37.130
There's parts of the
model that interface

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:40.410
with the fine-grained data.

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:42.890
So what happens is,
let's say, we're

00:37:42.890 --> 00:37:46.410
operating directly on
bytes or characters here.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:49.570
The sequence gets passed through
a lightweight encoder, which

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:53.650
will just be a generic sequence
model or several layers

00:37:53.650 --> 00:37:56.010
of a sequence model.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:58.370
Then there is a special
routing mechanism

00:37:58.370 --> 00:38:01.330
that for every single
character predicts,

00:38:01.330 --> 00:38:04.800
do I want this character
to be a boundary or not?

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:08.720
Or do I want it to be
the end of a chunk?

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:10.720
Then for the ones
that are decided

00:38:10.720 --> 00:38:14.200
to be chunks, which are
highlighted in the colors here,

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:17.920
we summarize each chunk
and then compress them down

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:19.553
into one representation.

00:38:19.553 --> 00:38:21.720
And so you can think of the
encoder and the chunking

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:25.780
as playing the same role as
the standard BPE tokenizer.

00:38:28.720 --> 00:38:31.520
And then once that
is compressed down,

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:34.080
then you have a much
shorter sequence,

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:37.240
then you pass this into
what we call the main model.

00:38:37.240 --> 00:38:39.480
And this model you can
just think of as, again,

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:42.820
a generic sequence model
that is effective on chunks.

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:44.920
And so here, for
example, by default,

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:47.040
we will just use a
transformer because they're

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:48.920
a tried and true model
that operate well

00:38:48.920 --> 00:38:53.200
on words or chunks or tokens.

00:38:53.200 --> 00:38:56.440
And so this is the inner stage.

00:38:56.440 --> 00:38:59.800
Then after your main model, you
take your output representations

00:38:59.800 --> 00:39:02.420
and then you expand them back
out to the original resolution

00:39:02.420 --> 00:39:04.290
and pass them through a decoder.

00:39:04.290 --> 00:39:06.650
And then do your autoregressive
prediction there.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:11.390
So at a very coarse level,
it's kind of, honestly,

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:14.470
similar to the existing pipeline
with a tokenizer and then

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:15.470
a transformer.

00:39:15.470 --> 00:39:17.590
Except the tokenization
happens inside the model

00:39:17.590 --> 00:39:19.830
through an encoder and
then a routing mechanism

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:21.090
to predict the boundaries.

00:39:24.155 --> 00:39:25.530
So there's a lot
of details here.

00:39:25.530 --> 00:39:27.447
But the one thing I'll
emphasize for this talk

00:39:27.447 --> 00:39:30.470
is that the outer stages
of this model, which

00:39:30.470 --> 00:39:35.390
are the ones that interface with
the byte-level data, strongly

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:37.390
benefit from being SSMs.

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.390
So just like the
previous experiment

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:43.590
I saw where if you run these
models on character-level data,

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:45.710
like an SSM versus
a transformer,

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:47.510
the SSM is way better.

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:50.550
Similarly here, you'll find
that any part of the model that

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:57.230
touches byte-level data strongly
benefits from being an SSM.

00:39:57.230 --> 00:40:00.230
And then outside
of this, there are

00:40:00.230 --> 00:40:02.740
lot of details in the
exact chunking mechanism

00:40:02.740 --> 00:40:04.493
and other parts
of the model that

00:40:04.493 --> 00:40:06.160
needed to be optimized
for this to work.

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:09.020
So this is a pretty
non-trivial problem,

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:11.940
because this is like a discrete
optimization problem that's

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:15.320
really unstable and difficult.
But if you get all right,

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:16.120
it actually works.

00:40:16.120 --> 00:40:17.780
And you actually
can train a model

00:40:17.780 --> 00:40:22.740
that operates fully
on tokenized data

00:40:22.740 --> 00:40:25.340
and actually seems to
scale pretty reasonably.

00:40:25.340 --> 00:40:32.780
And can actually outperform your
standard tokenized pipeline.

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:36.740
So these are some of
the results that we see.

00:40:36.740 --> 00:40:38.580
On the left here is,
again, a training

00:40:38.580 --> 00:40:41.300
plots where we're
fixing a model size

00:40:41.300 --> 00:40:44.740
and we're just showing how
the validation, perplexity

00:40:44.740 --> 00:40:50.900
or bits per byte decreases as
a function of the data seen.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:56.580
And one thing to also emphasize
is that here in this diagram,

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:59.840
the main stage I said can just
be a generic sequence model.

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:02.710
That means that the main stage
itself can be another H-Net.

00:41:02.710 --> 00:41:04.210
So that means that
you can basically

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:07.170
compose this model to have
multiple stages of chunking.

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:13.570
And so what H-Net that really
means is that it's not, per se,

00:41:13.570 --> 00:41:16.730
about tokenizer-free modeling.

00:41:16.730 --> 00:41:18.910
It is about dynamic chunking.

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:21.330
So it's about the idea
of taking any data

00:41:21.330 --> 00:41:24.330
and compressing it
into larger chunks that

00:41:24.330 --> 00:41:28.530
ideally represent more higher
level semantic meaning.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:30.550
And because it's done
completely end to end,

00:41:30.550 --> 00:41:32.810
this is the first
model that can not only

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:35.090
chunk things, but
also chunk things

00:41:35.090 --> 00:41:38.530
multiple times to get more and
more levels of abstraction.

00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:42.850
And what we see on
the plot on the left

00:41:42.850 --> 00:41:47.530
is that the transformer
baseline here is the black line.

00:41:47.530 --> 00:41:50.930
And then if you train that
H-Net with just a single stage

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:54.010
of chunking, like in
the previous diagram,

00:41:54.010 --> 00:41:56.570
it starts off worse
than the transformer.

00:41:56.570 --> 00:41:59.360
But after seeing enough
data, it crosses over

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:03.880
and seems to be scaling better.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:11.200
And the reason for this is that
basically, feature engineering

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:13.240
generally is a form
of inductive bias

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:17.080
that can help your model in
data constrained regimes,

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:19.125
but usually benefits
less from scaling.

00:42:19.125 --> 00:42:21.500
So this is the common theme
that we see in deep learning.

00:42:21.500 --> 00:42:23.240
This is the bitter lesson.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:25.560
And so we see this
exactly in this plot.

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:27.880
If you apply a stage
of chunking then

00:42:27.880 --> 00:42:30.360
it takes some amount of
data before the model learns

00:42:30.360 --> 00:42:34.720
how to create good chunks,
like we saw in the animation.

00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:36.640
But once it does, it
should theoretically

00:42:36.640 --> 00:42:39.360
be able to create better chunks
than what your hard-coded BP

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:41.720
algorithm gave you.

00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:43.120
And then if you
compose the model

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:46.320
by nesting it again and
doing two stages of chunking,

00:42:46.320 --> 00:42:47.940
the model seems to
scale even better.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:50.960
Although, there are
other challenges that

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:54.480
occur in this case, because
you have more parameters,

00:42:54.480 --> 00:42:56.520
it's harder to train and so on.

00:42:56.520 --> 00:42:59.350
But at least these
plots track what

00:42:59.350 --> 00:43:00.730
we would intuitively expect.

00:43:05.452 --> 00:43:06.910
It's not shown in
this plot, sorry.

00:43:06.910 --> 00:43:09.310
But you could also
ask yourself, what

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:13.790
happens if you run the H-Net
directly on BPE tokens?

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:17.050
And then the behavior kind of,
again, tracks what you expect.

00:43:17.050 --> 00:43:22.870
So compared to a H-Net on bytes,
BPE gives you some free boost

00:43:22.870 --> 00:43:25.510
because you're getting
the chunk of the model

00:43:25.510 --> 00:43:29.190
based on this kind of
hard-coded features.

00:43:29.190 --> 00:43:32.410
But if you just added another
stage of chunking to the H-Net

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:33.910
and operate on
bytes, then the model

00:43:33.910 --> 00:43:35.770
learns better features
than your BPE tokens.

00:43:35.770 --> 00:43:37.950
And this is what it looks like.

00:43:37.950 --> 00:43:40.110
And so if you look at these
comparisons, especially

00:43:40.110 --> 00:43:42.190
these two circled
in the red boxes,

00:43:42.190 --> 00:43:47.470
the theme is that no matter
what setting you're in,

00:43:47.470 --> 00:43:50.490
if you train a model
operating on BPE tokens,

00:43:50.490 --> 00:43:51.490
it works pretty good.

00:43:51.490 --> 00:43:54.430
But if you can manage
to throw away the BPE

00:43:54.430 --> 00:43:56.700
and learn these
chunks end to end,

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:58.200
then your model
will do even better.

00:44:00.940 --> 00:44:04.860
One variation of this that
is particularly interesting

00:44:04.860 --> 00:44:06.192
is that--

00:44:06.192 --> 00:44:08.400
so again, so each net is
just a generic architecture.

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:10.460
It doesn't have to
be applied on bytes.

00:44:10.460 --> 00:44:16.032
This plot shows what happens if
you apply it on the BPE data.

00:44:16.032 --> 00:44:17.740
And I think this one's
really interesting

00:44:17.740 --> 00:44:22.140
because it relates back to
this compression argument

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:23.780
that I talked about earlier.

00:44:23.780 --> 00:44:28.260
So what we did here is that
we applied a single stage

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:32.260
net to BPE tokens, and we
varied the architecture

00:44:32.260 --> 00:44:34.220
in the encoder and decoder.

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:36.500
So this plot,
basically, if we just

00:44:36.500 --> 00:44:38.780
look at this top
line and bottom line,

00:44:38.780 --> 00:44:45.152
this says that the encoder
is only transformer layers.

00:44:45.152 --> 00:44:47.860
The purple line and the red line
is where the encoder and decoder

00:44:47.860 --> 00:44:49.080
only layers.

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:53.300
And in between, you can vary
any combinations of these.

00:44:53.300 --> 00:44:55.970
And then what we see
here is that if you only

00:44:55.970 --> 00:44:58.810
have pure transformer layers
in the encoder and decoder,

00:44:58.810 --> 00:45:00.970
there is the model
is noticeably worse.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.970
And as soon as you start
putting in Mamba layers

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:07.170
into the encoder decoder,
model gets much better.

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:09.090
And so what is really
interesting here

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:14.890
is that, previously, we
said that transformers

00:45:14.890 --> 00:45:19.130
are great when your data
is already compressed,

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:21.210
like BPE tokens.

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:23.450
And they're less good in
your data is not compressed.

00:45:23.450 --> 00:45:26.590
So on characters, Mamba is
much better than transformers.

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:28.890
But in this experiment,
we are entirely

00:45:28.890 --> 00:45:32.130
operating over BPE tokens,
even in the outer stages.

00:45:32.130 --> 00:45:36.770
And so one might expect
that putting transformers

00:45:36.770 --> 00:45:39.090
in the outer layers is
completely fine, maybe

00:45:39.090 --> 00:45:42.592
even better because they're
only seeing BPE tokens.

00:45:42.592 --> 00:45:44.050
This plot is showing
that even when

00:45:44.050 --> 00:45:46.210
your compute
matching everything,

00:45:46.210 --> 00:45:48.410
it's better to put Mamba
in the outer stages.

00:45:48.410 --> 00:45:54.240
And so the lesson here to me is
that the benefit of these SSMs

00:45:54.240 --> 00:45:57.600
is not just about the resolution
of the data they're seeing,

00:45:57.600 --> 00:46:00.880
but also about this
implicit inductive

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:04.080
bias toward compression.

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:05.720
So I guess if you
look at the diagram

00:46:05.720 --> 00:46:08.280
here, the role of
the encoder layers

00:46:08.280 --> 00:46:11.940
is not just to interface with
the given resolution of data,

00:46:11.940 --> 00:46:13.920
but it's also passing
these representations

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:17.300
into the chunking layer which is
deciding how to chunk the data.

00:46:17.300 --> 00:46:19.000
And so you can imagine
that the encoder

00:46:19.000 --> 00:46:23.640
layers are playing some
role of compression as well.

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:25.240
Because literally,
the goal of it

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:28.000
is to try to produce
a literal compression

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:31.840
of the data in temporal sense.

00:46:31.840 --> 00:46:34.840
And so even when the data comes
not as characters but as BPE,

00:46:34.840 --> 00:46:37.673
it seems that applying
linear models--

00:46:37.673 --> 00:46:39.340
or you can call them
compressive models,

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:42.860
because, again of the fixed-size
state, it's very critical.

00:46:42.860 --> 00:46:45.600
And so this is I think,
the best evidence

00:46:45.600 --> 00:46:49.680
I've seen so far that
the finite-size state

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:53.290
of recurrent models is again,
not just for efficiency,

00:46:53.290 --> 00:46:55.830
but actually has fundamentally
different inductive biases.

00:46:55.830 --> 00:46:57.330
And perhaps in a
setting like this,

00:46:57.330 --> 00:46:59.230
it's actually doing
something like--

00:46:59.230 --> 00:47:01.430
I think of the
H-Net, the goal of it

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:03.990
is to create better
abstractions.

00:47:03.990 --> 00:47:05.290
That's the goal of chunking.

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:07.248
You want to take your
characters and chunk them

00:47:07.248 --> 00:47:08.730
into abstractions like words.

00:47:08.730 --> 00:47:10.105
You might want to
turn your words

00:47:10.105 --> 00:47:11.950
into phrases that have meaning.

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:13.670
And towards this
goal of creating

00:47:13.670 --> 00:47:15.430
abstractions, that's
fundamentally related

00:47:15.430 --> 00:47:16.430
to compression.

00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:18.390
And somehow these models
actually, empirically

00:47:18.390 --> 00:47:20.790
seem very important for that.

00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:22.870
A final result I'll
show on this topic

00:47:22.870 --> 00:47:28.002
is, again, you can apply these
to different types of data.

00:47:28.002 --> 00:47:29.710
This is, again, another
very recent paper

00:47:29.710 --> 00:47:32.470
that was just put on
archive a month ago,

00:47:32.470 --> 00:47:36.670
where we tried applying a
couple of different established

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:39.390
architectures to
DNA modeling again.

00:47:39.390 --> 00:47:40.870
And once again,
DNA is interesting

00:47:40.870 --> 00:47:44.590
because in language,
modeling characters is

00:47:44.590 --> 00:47:45.970
a bit of an academic exercise.

00:47:45.970 --> 00:47:50.620
It's a long-term direction
because we do have tokenizers

00:47:50.620 --> 00:47:53.500
and most people are like, why
not just use the tokenizer?

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:56.112
In DNA, it's actually really
difficult to define tokenizers,

00:47:56.112 --> 00:47:57.820
at least in a semantically
meaningful way

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:00.900
like you can for English.

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:03.220
And we see even bigger
gaps, generally,

00:48:03.220 --> 00:48:06.340
for these kind of
alternative models.

00:48:06.340 --> 00:48:09.780
This plot here shows instead
of just fixing a model

00:48:09.780 --> 00:48:12.040
and running out
the training curve,

00:48:12.040 --> 00:48:14.180
this is actually showing
formal scaling laws.

00:48:14.180 --> 00:48:16.580
So each point here is a
different flop budget.

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:23.500
And then the model size
to data amount was swept.

00:48:23.500 --> 00:48:28.340
So these are standard
scaling laws, but for DNA.

00:48:28.340 --> 00:48:33.620
And we found remarkably
linear trends here.

00:48:33.620 --> 00:48:37.100
And what's interesting, again,
is that these trends actually

00:48:37.100 --> 00:48:39.820
are so different from language
that usually in language

00:48:39.820 --> 00:48:43.940
these lines are just shifted
and you get a constant magnitude

00:48:43.940 --> 00:48:44.900
of improvement.

00:48:44.900 --> 00:48:49.840
But when it comes
to DNA, the H-Net

00:48:49.840 --> 00:48:52.090
is actually discovering
patterns that you can't really

00:48:52.090 --> 00:48:55.610
find with off-the-shelf
tokenizers,

00:48:55.610 --> 00:49:00.130
and it's actually scaling
fundamentally better.

00:49:00.130 --> 00:49:04.370
So hopefully, all of these
have painted the picture

00:49:04.370 --> 00:49:07.690
that the different
types of models

00:49:07.690 --> 00:49:09.670
really are doing very
different things.

00:49:09.670 --> 00:49:12.690
They have very different
inductive biases.

00:49:12.690 --> 00:49:15.150
And this goes back to my
main thesis that I claimed,

00:49:15.150 --> 00:49:19.043
which is that transformers
work really, really well.

00:49:19.043 --> 00:49:21.210
But they're particularly
effective when the data has

00:49:21.210 --> 00:49:22.930
already been encoded properly.

00:49:22.930 --> 00:49:24.970
And you often need
different types

00:49:24.970 --> 00:49:27.490
of models when your
data is not encoded

00:49:27.490 --> 00:49:29.748
or it's very
difficult to encode.

00:49:29.748 --> 00:49:31.290
And so I think the
future of modeling

00:49:31.290 --> 00:49:33.510
will require not
just transformers,

00:49:33.510 --> 00:49:37.130
but a lot of other ideas.

00:49:37.130 --> 00:49:39.610
So far, most people
have viewed transformers

00:49:39.610 --> 00:49:41.565
as basically just like
a very powerful tool.

00:49:41.565 --> 00:49:43.190
We don't really look
at it or touch it,

00:49:43.190 --> 00:49:45.290
and we designed the
whole pipeline around it

00:49:45.290 --> 00:49:48.500
instead through a lot
of feature engineering,

00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:52.310
through the way we process
the data and so on.

00:49:52.310 --> 00:49:54.560
But hopefully, I've kind of
conveyed that there really

00:49:54.560 --> 00:49:56.700
is a lot of different
alternatives out there.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:49:58.880
There's really a lot of
room for improvement.

00:49:58.880 --> 00:50:01.320
I personally believe that
there's actually still

00:50:01.320 --> 00:50:04.160
a lot of room for major
improvements in architecture

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.180
design.

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:10.370
So just to come back now
to the title of the talk

00:50:10.370 --> 00:50:11.120
on the trade offs.

00:50:11.120 --> 00:50:13.800
Just to summarize.

00:50:13.800 --> 00:50:19.680
SSMs are largely thought of
as a alternative model that

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:22.360
is more efficient
than transformers,

00:50:22.360 --> 00:50:25.040
but potentially more weak.

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:27.260
So just by the way
they're defined,

00:50:27.260 --> 00:50:29.400
they're obviously
more efficient.

00:50:29.400 --> 00:50:32.880
But it's often pointed out the
specific problems they have,

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:36.560
especially with
retrieval-like abilities.

00:50:36.560 --> 00:50:38.240
But I actually think
that the efficiency

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:40.160
is a bit of a red herring.

00:50:40.160 --> 00:50:41.980
These models are not
just more efficient,

00:50:41.980 --> 00:50:44.940
but they're actually doing
different form of modeling.

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:48.030
So again, I think of
them as being a stateful

00:50:48.030 --> 00:50:49.250
and a compressive model.

00:50:49.250 --> 00:50:54.310
And these lead to very
different types of benefits

00:50:54.310 --> 00:50:57.270
that they have from things like
state tracking, which I didn't

00:50:57.270 --> 00:51:01.430
talk about today, to things like
building abstractions, which

00:51:01.430 --> 00:51:04.270
we saw through the H-Net.

00:51:04.270 --> 00:51:06.670
And both of these pros
and cons are two sides

00:51:06.670 --> 00:51:09.830
of the same coin, which is the
way it's autoregressive state is

00:51:09.830 --> 00:51:14.190
defined in terms of
this brain-like state.

00:51:14.190 --> 00:51:17.310
Similarly, attention,
the strengths

00:51:17.310 --> 00:51:20.670
are that it gets the
ability to pay attention

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:24.367
to very specific
details in its context.

00:51:24.367 --> 00:51:26.450
It's exceptionally strong
at recall and retrieval,

00:51:26.450 --> 00:51:29.070
which is where SSMs struggle.

00:51:29.070 --> 00:51:30.830
And usually people
think of the downside

00:51:30.830 --> 00:51:36.078
as the efficiency, the
quadratic scaling of attention.

00:51:36.078 --> 00:51:37.870
But once again, I think
that the efficiency

00:51:37.870 --> 00:51:39.150
is a bit of a red herring.

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:42.630
And the way I really
think about attention

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:45.460
is that the downside
is that it is highly

00:51:45.460 --> 00:51:47.860
dependent on the resolution
and the semantic meaning

00:51:47.860 --> 00:51:50.220
of the data.

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:53.040
Through the examples we saw,
when you change the resolution,

00:51:53.040 --> 00:51:57.500
even in language modeling
from BPE tokens to bytes,

00:51:57.500 --> 00:52:00.900
the performance of attention
changes dramatically.

00:52:00.900 --> 00:52:03.020
And another way to put
this is that attention

00:52:03.020 --> 00:52:04.940
is beholden to the
tokens it's given.

00:52:04.940 --> 00:52:09.940
Because of the way it's kind of
caching every single token it

00:52:09.940 --> 00:52:13.660
means that it's very sensitive
to the resolution of the tokens

00:52:13.660 --> 00:52:14.700
it's given.

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:18.122
It has no ability to change
what a token means to it.

00:52:18.122 --> 00:52:19.580
And once again,
these pros and cons

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:22.700
are both two sides of
the same coin, which

00:52:22.700 --> 00:52:25.740
is the way that it's
autoregressive state is defined

00:52:25.740 --> 00:52:30.620
through this
database-like cache.

00:52:30.620 --> 00:52:35.140
And so I think that the
efficiency arguments

00:52:35.140 --> 00:52:38.200
for both of these
models are, again,

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:39.500
a little bit of a distraction.

00:52:39.500 --> 00:52:42.140
Because at the end
of the day, we just

00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:44.970
want the models to model
the things we want.

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:48.678
And the exact efficiency
there actually-- it's

00:52:48.678 --> 00:52:49.970
not the right thing to look at.

00:52:49.970 --> 00:52:53.330
Because there may be tasks, for
example, where you really do

00:52:53.330 --> 00:52:55.548
need the quadratic attention.

00:52:55.548 --> 00:52:57.090
There may be tasks
where you actually

00:52:57.090 --> 00:52:59.710
have to cache and memorize every
single thing in your context,

00:52:59.710 --> 00:53:01.010
and you can't get around that.

00:53:01.010 --> 00:53:02.510
And then so there's
no point arguing

00:53:02.510 --> 00:53:03.990
that the quadratic
scaling is bad.

00:53:06.610 --> 00:53:09.450
And to give a
final picture here.

00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:11.810
So one way to think about
architecture design in general

00:53:11.810 --> 00:53:16.090
is that there's a
picture where we

00:53:16.090 --> 00:53:18.810
think of models
as this black box,

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:23.610
where during training, you
give it compute and data.

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:27.210
And then you create
an intelligent model

00:53:27.210 --> 00:53:31.330
that has a wide range of
capabilities on the other side.

00:53:31.330 --> 00:53:34.650
And in this picture,
you basically

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:37.090
think of your model
training as a vehicle

00:53:37.090 --> 00:53:41.760
that converts compute or
flops into intelligence

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:43.920
or capabilities.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:47.120
And what you want is to find
the most efficient vehicle that

00:53:47.120 --> 00:53:50.200
does this conversion.

00:53:50.200 --> 00:53:54.960
And so the central
question to ask is simply,

00:53:54.960 --> 00:53:59.680
is my model using every
single flop it's given wisely?

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:05.000
And so again, there are
settings where you actually

00:54:05.000 --> 00:54:06.720
need to spend a lot
of compute in order

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:08.740
to do certain capabilities.

00:54:08.740 --> 00:54:10.600
But there are also
settings where

00:54:10.600 --> 00:54:13.520
your model is doing a lot of
compute that doesn't really

00:54:13.520 --> 00:54:14.100
make sense.

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:16.980
For example, if your transformer
is given character-level data,

00:54:16.980 --> 00:54:19.147
it doesn't need to be caching
every single character

00:54:19.147 --> 00:54:21.000
into its KV cache.

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:23.500
And so this is the central
question of architecture design

00:54:23.500 --> 00:54:25.760
is designing better
and better black boxes

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:27.800
here that perform
this conversion.

00:54:27.800 --> 00:54:31.080
And I think there's still a lot
of room for improvement there.

00:54:31.080 --> 00:54:35.560
OK, that's the end of the talk.

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:37.636
Questions?

00:54:37.636 --> 00:54:41.122
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:46.810
OK, awesome.

00:54:46.810 --> 00:54:48.990
Thanks, Albert, for the
very insightful talk.

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:51.750
We're going to be soliciting
some questions now.

00:54:51.750 --> 00:54:54.550
We're going to be balancing
between in-person questions

00:54:54.550 --> 00:54:56.670
and those online.

00:54:56.670 --> 00:54:59.070
So we can get started with
some in-person questions

00:54:59.070 --> 00:55:01.170
if anybody here has any.

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:09.810
Thank you.

00:55:09.810 --> 00:55:11.370
I really admire your work.

00:55:11.370 --> 00:55:13.450
I'm a PhD in Chris
Ray's lab as well.

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:16.010
And we are constantly looking
through all your old papers,

00:55:16.010 --> 00:55:18.150
so I really appreciate it.

00:55:18.150 --> 00:55:23.310
A lot of the current
SSM inspiration

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:28.670
is trying to trade off
some recurrent expressivity

00:55:28.670 --> 00:55:32.150
for trainability under the
condition of using backprop

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:33.210
or backprop through time.

00:55:33.210 --> 00:55:35.050
Which you mentioned the
brain a bunch of times,

00:55:35.050 --> 00:55:36.675
there's very little
evidence that we're

00:55:36.675 --> 00:55:39.860
keeping C copies of the brain,
and doing backprop through time.

00:55:39.860 --> 00:55:42.380
It would be very unrealistic
that we're doing that.

00:55:42.380 --> 00:55:44.620
So there must exist
some other solver

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:47.420
that is happening inside
your brain right now.

00:55:47.420 --> 00:55:48.750
What are your thoughts on that?

00:55:48.750 --> 00:55:50.000
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:55:50.000 --> 00:55:54.620
So I'm actually a big fan
of some of these works

00:55:54.620 --> 00:55:59.060
that try to look at completely
different computation paradigms.

00:55:59.060 --> 00:56:03.020
I think a lot of what drives
my work is the idea that we are

00:56:03.020 --> 00:56:07.220
probably in some of local
optimum in the entire way

00:56:07.220 --> 00:56:09.420
that all of our
training is set up

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:12.380
from the architecture, which is
where a lot of this is focused.

00:56:12.380 --> 00:56:16.740
But also even to bigger things
like backprop and so on.

00:56:16.740 --> 00:56:18.740
It is also not clear
to me that backprop

00:56:18.740 --> 00:56:20.600
is the best thing to do.

00:56:24.448 --> 00:56:26.740
There's a lot of co-dependence
between the model design

00:56:26.740 --> 00:56:29.700
and just the physical
hardware we have

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:32.220
and the realities
of these things.

00:56:32.220 --> 00:56:34.960
I personally haven't thought
that much about backprop myself.

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:39.050
But I have thought about, it
could cause serious issues

00:56:39.050 --> 00:56:44.038
with-- for example, one issue
is it causes a physical memory

00:56:44.038 --> 00:56:45.830
constraint on your
long-range dependencies.

00:56:45.830 --> 00:56:47.310
Because if you're
using backprop,

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:49.630
then if you can't fit your
entire sequence in memory,

00:56:49.630 --> 00:56:52.210
you literally can't
learn dependencies.

00:56:52.210 --> 00:56:54.162
You can cook up
pathological examples

00:56:54.162 --> 00:56:56.370
where you just literally
can never learn dependencies

00:56:56.370 --> 00:56:57.650
that are long enough.

00:56:57.650 --> 00:56:59.748
So it could be a problem.

00:56:59.748 --> 00:57:02.290
It could be the case that for
any realistic task that we care

00:57:02.290 --> 00:57:05.070
about, we can engineer
it to be good enough,

00:57:05.070 --> 00:57:08.170
which is the way
the world's going.

00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:11.876
But I do think that there
are probably other--

00:57:11.876 --> 00:57:15.810
getting out of the local optimum
and doing much bigger changes

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:16.810
in these models.

00:57:16.810 --> 00:57:19.185
Another example is
that, as I mentioned,

00:57:19.185 --> 00:57:20.810
a lot of the work
that's been done here

00:57:20.810 --> 00:57:24.490
is on making these models
efficient on current hardware,

00:57:24.490 --> 00:57:27.290
like GPUs, you
actually sacrifice

00:57:27.290 --> 00:57:29.690
a bunch of expressivity
that you could gain back

00:57:29.690 --> 00:57:31.168
by adding true recurrence.

00:57:31.168 --> 00:57:33.710
And there has been another line
of work on trying to do this,

00:57:33.710 --> 00:57:36.387
but it really struggles because
it's just so hard to compute.

00:57:36.387 --> 00:57:38.720
I actually think that that's
another line of work that's

00:57:38.720 --> 00:57:39.500
quite promising.

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:43.240
And I have some of
my own ideas there.

00:57:43.240 --> 00:57:46.080
Again, you might be able to
get fundamentally better models

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:49.080
by completely reimagining
the constraints

00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:50.140
that we currently have.

00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:54.380
Great.

00:57:54.380 --> 00:57:56.260
Any other in-person questions?

00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:03.875
Thanks for the amazing talk.

00:58:03.875 --> 00:58:06.000
Yeah, it was really
interesting to see your results

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:09.740
with H-net and the dynamic
chunking mechanism.

00:58:09.740 --> 00:58:12.720
And yeah, it's really cool
that it gets better performance

00:58:12.720 --> 00:58:14.380
on DNA sequence
modeling and stuff.

00:58:14.380 --> 00:58:16.480
I was curious if you
feel the chunking

00:58:16.480 --> 00:58:19.440
mechanism is elegant and
will play a substantial role

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:21.297
in future models?

00:58:21.297 --> 00:58:22.380
Yeah, so it's hard to say.

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:26.520
So we worked on this, which
took a long time because I

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:28.920
did fundamentally
believe that chunking

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:31.840
is a really critical primitive
that will lead to better

00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:35.140
models in the long term.

00:58:35.140 --> 00:58:39.000
The version that we published
is just the first step there.

00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:42.260
I think it was the first really
end-to-end model that works.

00:58:42.260 --> 00:58:44.983
I don't think that is
the end game at all.

00:58:44.983 --> 00:58:46.900
There are improvements
that you could probably

00:58:46.900 --> 00:58:48.940
make to all parts
of it, including

00:58:48.940 --> 00:58:52.460
the chunking mechanism itself.

00:58:52.460 --> 00:58:54.140
And I think, so far,
it has not really

00:58:54.140 --> 00:58:56.000
been validated at larger scales.

00:58:58.820 --> 00:59:02.300
The way I think about it is
that, philosophically even,

00:59:02.300 --> 00:59:04.620
it just feels like it's
so important to learn

00:59:04.620 --> 00:59:06.555
these things from
scratch that I do

00:59:06.555 --> 00:59:09.180
think that coming up with better
and better chunking mechanisms

00:59:09.180 --> 00:59:11.160
could be really important
for future models.

00:59:21.020 --> 00:59:22.820
Thank you.

00:59:22.820 --> 00:59:24.680
So just one technical question.

00:59:24.680 --> 00:59:28.220
I'm curious, since you're
setting the separators

00:59:28.220 --> 00:59:31.700
at arbitrary points,
typically with a tokenizer

00:59:31.700 --> 00:59:33.650
you have a set
dictionary, so I'm

00:59:33.650 --> 00:59:39.810
curious how you feed that
sequence in when the sequence

00:59:39.810 --> 00:59:43.610
chunks can vary
very dynamically.

00:59:43.610 --> 00:59:47.530
But I guess more generally,
you've drawn several comparisons

00:59:47.530 --> 00:59:50.390
to how the human brain works.

00:59:50.390 --> 00:59:55.010
And this is something I'm also
interested in, this aspect of

00:59:55.010 --> 01:00:00.550
are we doing some of dynamic
like analysis of incoming data?

01:00:00.550 --> 01:00:05.610
What data do we need to
remember or compress or curate?

01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:10.450
And I've been interested
in that towards improving

01:00:10.450 --> 01:00:13.610
the amount of memory that
models have access to.

01:00:13.610 --> 01:00:16.090
So I'm curious what
your perspective would

01:00:16.090 --> 01:00:20.590
be on using these types
of state-based models

01:00:20.590 --> 01:00:27.250
perhaps to curate memories or
curate more efficient memory

01:00:27.250 --> 01:00:31.120
for an attention-based
or a lookup-based model

01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:34.480
to use down the line to
extend the amount that we're

01:00:34.480 --> 01:00:39.683
able to actually memorize
in a continuous interaction.

01:00:39.683 --> 01:00:41.100
All right, so two
questions there.

01:00:41.100 --> 01:00:42.808
So first, I think the
first one was about

01:00:42.808 --> 01:00:46.100
a little more mechanically
how the model works.

01:00:46.100 --> 01:00:50.740
So this is actually one
key advantage of the model.

01:00:50.740 --> 01:00:53.360
So I think the
question is about--

01:00:53.360 --> 01:00:55.960
normally when you use
BPE you have a fixed

01:00:55.960 --> 01:00:58.640
vocabulary and each
vocabulary, you'll

01:00:58.640 --> 01:01:01.120
look up an embedding
to pass to your model.

01:01:01.120 --> 01:01:03.200
One of the challenges
of tokenized-free models

01:01:03.200 --> 01:01:05.300
is that if you have an
infinite size vocabulary,

01:01:05.300 --> 01:01:08.600
you can't have
infinite embeddings.

01:01:08.600 --> 01:01:10.487
So what happens here
is that we don't

01:01:10.487 --> 01:01:12.320
need to think about
those embeddings at all.

01:01:12.320 --> 01:01:16.180
Because everything is happening
inside the model end to end,

01:01:16.180 --> 01:01:18.040
there is no lookup
table at all once you

01:01:18.040 --> 01:01:20.837
get past the very
first encoding stage.

01:01:20.837 --> 01:01:22.920
So here, you think of this
model as just operating

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:25.740
on a vocabulary of whatever
fine-grained units you have,

01:01:25.740 --> 01:01:29.590
which might be, a size
of 256 if it's a byte.

01:01:29.590 --> 01:01:31.292
You get embeddings here.

01:01:31.292 --> 01:01:32.750
Then you pass this
into your model.

01:01:32.750 --> 01:01:34.270
And at every future
stage, you're

01:01:34.270 --> 01:01:36.710
just working in embedding space.

01:01:36.710 --> 01:01:40.070
And so for example, the
you create this embedding,

01:01:40.070 --> 01:01:41.630
the simplest way is just--

01:01:41.630 --> 01:01:45.310
so let's say this embedding
corresponds to this chunk here.

01:01:45.310 --> 01:01:48.290
The simplest way is just to
literally just copy and paste,

01:01:48.290 --> 01:01:50.990
whatever this embedding
is on the other side just

01:01:50.990 --> 01:01:54.490
gets passed through and we
ignore all the ones in between.

01:01:54.490 --> 01:01:58.593
Or you can just pull
over this chunk here.

01:01:58.593 --> 01:02:00.510
So basically, at the end
of the encoder stage,

01:02:00.510 --> 01:02:02.963
you have embeddings
in some latent space.

01:02:02.963 --> 01:02:05.130
And then you decide where
you want the chunks to be.

01:02:05.130 --> 01:02:07.505
And you're embedding that gets
passed into the main stage

01:02:07.505 --> 01:02:10.627
is just, say, pooling
over that chunk.

01:02:10.627 --> 01:02:12.210
The second question
is about, I think,

01:02:12.210 --> 01:02:15.670
the analogy to brains and so on.

01:02:15.670 --> 01:02:19.790
To be clear, these analogies
are very, very coarse.

01:02:19.790 --> 01:02:21.910
I'm not a neuroscientist.

01:02:21.910 --> 01:02:24.630
They might yell at me if
they heard these analogies.

01:02:24.630 --> 01:02:28.060
But I think, a lot of
this is just inspiration.

01:02:28.060 --> 01:02:29.900
I use humans more as
a proof of concept

01:02:29.900 --> 01:02:33.980
of what things should be
possible with intelligence,

01:02:33.980 --> 01:02:36.460
and what are some really
high-level ways that these

01:02:36.460 --> 01:02:39.920
might be achievable.

01:02:39.920 --> 01:02:41.380
I mean, I will say
that chunking, I

01:02:41.380 --> 01:02:43.255
think is a concept that
was directly inspired

01:02:43.255 --> 01:02:45.680
by cognitive psychology.

01:02:48.700 --> 01:02:50.460
Actually, one of the
things I do think

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:54.260
it will be useful for is kind of
as a more clever way of creating

01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:56.400
these databases or caches.

01:02:56.400 --> 01:02:59.740
Because one of the key
challenges of long context

01:02:59.740 --> 01:03:03.380
is knowing what things do
you store or not again.

01:03:03.380 --> 01:03:06.060
And I think that one of
the potential, for example,

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:10.380
use cases of things like H-Nets
is exploring their potential

01:03:10.380 --> 01:03:11.520
for long context modeling.

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:14.260
Because what you can
do is you can imagine

01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:16.220
if you can keep
iterating this, then you

01:03:16.220 --> 01:03:18.940
can basically try to
compress your context

01:03:18.940 --> 01:03:22.260
into higher and higher level
abstractions or memories that

01:03:22.260 --> 01:03:24.800
are like core summaries
of this thing.

01:03:24.800 --> 01:03:29.170
But then allows an inner model
to attend over much fewer things

01:03:29.170 --> 01:03:31.755
and in a much kind
high-level way.

01:03:31.755 --> 01:03:33.130
So I think that's
really directly

01:03:33.130 --> 01:03:35.530
an inspiration for this.

01:03:35.530 --> 01:03:38.550
I don't have a further progress
on that particular idea,

01:03:38.550 --> 01:03:40.490
but I do think it's
clearly related

01:03:40.490 --> 01:03:45.690
to the whole philosophy of
why one might want to do this.

01:03:45.690 --> 01:03:49.530
I'll read a couple
questions from online.

01:03:49.530 --> 01:03:52.890
Someone asked, do SSMs
outperform transformers

01:03:52.890 --> 01:03:56.350
in data-constrained or small
language model settings,

01:03:56.350 --> 01:04:00.530
or vice versa, given their
different inductive biases?

01:04:00.530 --> 01:04:01.350
Good question.

01:04:01.350 --> 01:04:04.770
I don't the answer to that
off the top of my head.

01:04:04.770 --> 01:04:06.310
Yeah, I think for
language modeling,

01:04:06.310 --> 01:04:08.933
a lot of people
care about scaling.

01:04:08.933 --> 01:04:09.850
I actually don't know.

01:04:09.850 --> 01:04:12.990
I will say, I found out a little
tidbit of information yesterday,

01:04:12.990 --> 01:04:16.650
which is I think OpenAI recently
released this parameter golf

01:04:16.650 --> 01:04:17.150
challenge.

01:04:17.150 --> 01:04:18.395
If you guys have seen it.

01:04:18.395 --> 01:04:20.770
But people have been trying
to train really small models.

01:04:20.770 --> 01:04:22.330
And I saw that
somebody was trying

01:04:22.330 --> 01:04:24.150
to train a Mamba 3
base model there,

01:04:24.150 --> 01:04:27.400
which was doing decently well.

01:04:27.400 --> 01:04:30.820
But they had a lot of
ablations on their submission.

01:04:30.820 --> 01:04:33.440
And one thing they said is that,
even though the model works

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:36.560
well, at least including
a little bit of attention

01:04:36.560 --> 01:04:38.680
was actually pretty
critical when you

01:04:38.680 --> 01:04:40.680
have these really tiny models.

01:04:40.680 --> 01:04:44.240
That's actually something
I didn't about before.

01:04:44.240 --> 01:04:45.860
So I don't really
know the answer,

01:04:45.860 --> 01:04:48.320
but that's as far as I know.

01:04:48.320 --> 01:04:49.720
One last one.

01:04:49.720 --> 01:04:51.960
With transformers, people
look at attention maps

01:04:51.960 --> 01:04:53.920
as a form of interpretability.

01:04:53.920 --> 01:04:57.960
Is there a similar work or proxy
for SSMs given the information

01:04:57.960 --> 01:04:59.440
compression?

01:04:59.440 --> 01:05:02.475
I think this one is harder.

01:05:02.475 --> 01:05:04.100
I don't think I have
the slides for it,

01:05:04.100 --> 01:05:07.240
but there is a correspondence
between some flavors

01:05:07.240 --> 01:05:10.200
of SSMs and attention.

01:05:10.200 --> 01:05:13.220
So I guess as I mentioned
at the very beginning,

01:05:13.220 --> 01:05:15.460
there's many names
for these models.

01:05:15.460 --> 01:05:17.580
And one of the names
is linear attention.

01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:19.960
So there's a much deeper
connection between SSMs

01:05:19.960 --> 01:05:21.180
and linear attention.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:23.640
And that's implicitly
related to attention.

01:05:23.640 --> 01:05:27.150
But at the end of the day,
basically one thing you can do

01:05:27.150 --> 01:05:30.950
is visualize an
attention-like map.

01:05:30.950 --> 01:05:32.530
It's not literally
attention matrix,

01:05:32.530 --> 01:05:36.390
but basically you can visualize
the dependence on every token

01:05:36.390 --> 01:05:39.890
with every other token in
a similar way to attention.

01:05:43.503 --> 01:05:45.170
I mean, it's basically
an attention map,

01:05:45.170 --> 01:05:47.833
and you can do the
same comparisons.

01:05:47.833 --> 01:05:49.750
And it's actually pretty
revealing of the type

01:05:49.750 --> 01:05:51.170
of behavior that it can do.

01:05:51.170 --> 01:05:54.690
So for example, that
visualization, for example,

01:05:54.690 --> 01:05:58.030
shows that attention
sometimes can really

01:05:58.030 --> 01:06:00.230
act like hard attention
and only pay attention

01:06:00.230 --> 01:06:03.470
to specific tokens, while
SSMs generally always

01:06:03.470 --> 01:06:05.310
kind of squish
everything together

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:08.230
and it can be pretty diffuse.

01:06:08.230 --> 01:06:12.950
This is probably not the only
visualization that's relevant.

01:06:12.950 --> 01:06:16.190
But yeah, I think a lot of
this is ongoing work, too,

01:06:16.190 --> 01:06:17.710
do more mechanistic
interpretability

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:19.990
of these models.

01:06:19.990 --> 01:06:20.490
OK, great.

01:06:20.490 --> 01:06:23.080
Let's give another round of
