01:06:41.600 --> 01:06:44.420
I promise I'll be very quick,
about 10 minutes or so.

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:45.560
But hi, everyone.

01:06:45.560 --> 01:06:47.400
My name is Anaiya Raisinghani.

01:06:47.400 --> 01:06:50.860
I'm a senior technical
evangelist at MongoDB.

01:06:50.860 --> 01:06:54.980
And we are so excited to be
sponsoring CS25 this quarter.

01:06:54.980 --> 01:06:58.460
And I'm here right now because
there's a genuinely interesting

01:06:58.460 --> 01:07:01.200
engineering problem that
I think is worth about 10

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:02.580
minutes of your time.

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:06.060
And because MongoDB happens to
be a really great way of solving

01:07:06.060 --> 01:07:06.872
it.

01:07:06.872 --> 01:07:08.580
So you're all in this
room because you're

01:07:08.580 --> 01:07:12.000
thinking about transformers,
about multimodal models,

01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:14.460
and about what comes next in AI.

01:07:14.460 --> 01:07:17.740
Here's what I want to help
add to that conversation.

01:07:17.740 --> 01:07:21.050
The latest multimodal models
have gotten really good

01:07:21.050 --> 01:07:24.930
at understanding a document
the way a human being does.

01:07:24.930 --> 01:07:28.090
The question now is whether
your data infrastructure

01:07:28.090 --> 01:07:30.113
is accurately keeping up.

01:07:30.113 --> 01:07:31.530
And today, I want
to show you what

01:07:31.530 --> 01:07:34.730
it looks like when the
model and the database

01:07:34.730 --> 01:07:37.130
are actually designed
to work together.

01:07:37.130 --> 01:07:39.610
So I'm going to start off
with a concrete scenario,

01:07:39.610 --> 01:07:43.190
walk you through why the
standard approach breaks down.

01:07:43.190 --> 01:07:45.690
And then show you what a better
architecture actually looks

01:07:45.690 --> 01:07:47.210
like end to end.

01:07:47.210 --> 01:07:49.930
So let's get into it.

01:07:49.930 --> 01:07:52.290
So first off, let's
set the scene.

01:07:52.290 --> 01:07:54.730
We're going to be talking
about an insurance company.

01:07:54.730 --> 01:07:58.090
Not the sexiest example,
but a really good example.

01:07:58.090 --> 01:08:01.570
So let's say that your insurance
company gets about 10,000 claims

01:08:01.570 --> 01:08:02.290
a day.

01:08:02.290 --> 01:08:04.970
And each one of these
claims is a PDF.

01:08:04.970 --> 01:08:08.810
And these claims include
damage photos, tables of repair

01:08:08.810 --> 01:08:10.890
estimates, signatures.

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:15.170
And the key here is that no
two claims look the same.

01:08:15.170 --> 01:08:16.850
This is actually
a real dashboard

01:08:16.850 --> 01:08:19.303
that was built by MongoDB.

01:08:19.303 --> 01:08:20.720
And what you're
seeing on the left

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:23.620
is an active claim, which
is a smashed windshield.

01:08:23.620 --> 01:08:25.479
On the right, claims like this.

01:08:25.479 --> 01:08:28.520
Five similar claims are
pulled back instantly

01:08:28.520 --> 01:08:31.880
with loss amounts, dates,
and notes attached.

01:08:31.880 --> 01:08:35.240
And the adjuster simply asked,
"show me crashes like this one,"

01:08:35.240 --> 01:08:37.920
and the system knew
exactly what to return.

01:08:37.920 --> 01:08:39.779
So the question is,
how do you build that?

01:08:39.779 --> 01:08:41.800
And how do you build
that with retrieving back

01:08:41.800 --> 01:08:43.234
accurate information?

01:08:43.234 --> 01:08:45.359
Because the hard part, as
most of us probably know,

01:08:45.359 --> 01:08:48.680
isn't the search, it's
what happens to those PDFs

01:08:48.680 --> 01:08:52.120
before retrieval even starts.

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:56.200
And until recently, the standard
approach looked a lot like this.

01:08:56.200 --> 01:09:00.760
You would OCR the document and
extract whatever text you could,

01:09:00.760 --> 01:09:04.859
chunk it into pieces, pass those
chunks to an embedding model,

01:09:04.859 --> 01:09:07.600
store the vectors, and
then retrieve a query.

01:09:07.600 --> 01:09:12.080
And this works completely fine
for very clean text documents.

01:09:12.080 --> 01:09:13.859
But how many
documents out there,

01:09:13.859 --> 01:09:17.089
especially when you're dealing
with insurance claims, health

01:09:17.089 --> 01:09:21.830
care, law, how many of those
are truly, perfectly clean text?

01:09:21.830 --> 01:09:26.029
A lot of context can get lost
even before you start searching.

01:09:26.029 --> 01:09:28.510
And the moment you
OCR a damaged photo,

01:09:28.510 --> 01:09:31.130
the tables in the documents,
the handwritten notes,

01:09:31.130 --> 01:09:33.510
so much important
information, that

01:09:33.510 --> 01:09:36.670
can completely alter the whole
trajectory of your claim.

01:09:36.670 --> 01:09:38.590
It can be accently omitted.

01:09:38.590 --> 01:09:40.189
And in situations
like these, you

01:09:40.189 --> 01:09:44.710
don't want any crucial
information to be left out.

01:09:44.710 --> 01:09:47.710
So Vision RAG makes
one fundamental change.

01:09:47.710 --> 01:09:50.550
Instead of parsing the
document into text first,

01:09:50.550 --> 01:09:53.229
you actually embed
the entire page itself

01:09:53.229 --> 01:09:55.870
as a single multimodal vector.

01:09:55.870 --> 01:09:58.390
So let's chat about the
new pipeline a little bit.

01:09:58.390 --> 01:10:00.770
You have text and image
documents that come in.

01:10:00.770 --> 01:10:02.830
They go straight to a
multi-modal embedding

01:10:02.830 --> 01:10:04.990
model with no OCR step.

01:10:04.990 --> 01:10:08.390
The references and embeddings
go straight into MongoDB Atlas

01:10:08.390 --> 01:10:09.790
as your vector store.

01:10:09.790 --> 01:10:11.870
And then at query
time, the same model

01:10:11.870 --> 01:10:15.870
is able to embed the query and
search, finds the closest pages.

01:10:15.870 --> 01:10:19.300
And a vision capable LM
reads the actual images

01:10:19.300 --> 01:10:22.180
to help generate your answer.

01:10:22.180 --> 01:10:23.260
So what makes this work?

01:10:23.260 --> 01:10:26.280
And have any of you in the
audience heard of Voyage AI.

01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:30.350
I know Tengyu is a professor
here, so maybe some of you have.

01:10:30.350 --> 01:10:31.100
No worries if not.

01:10:31.100 --> 01:10:33.300
You can look him up after.

01:10:33.300 --> 01:10:34.240
So how does this work?

01:10:34.240 --> 01:10:36.020
But to ensure your
important data

01:10:36.020 --> 01:10:38.580
isn't getting omitted
in the process,

01:10:38.580 --> 01:10:41.260
it all really comes down
to the embedding model.

01:10:41.260 --> 01:10:44.660
So with earlier
multimodal models

01:10:44.660 --> 01:10:46.880
which were CLIP-based
architectures,

01:10:46.880 --> 01:10:49.700
they ran separate encoders
for text and images.

01:10:49.700 --> 01:10:52.960
So vision trunk on one
side, text on the other.

01:10:52.960 --> 01:10:54.940
And then they were
just routed together.

01:10:54.940 --> 01:10:58.100
The problem is that those
outputs are very independently

01:10:58.100 --> 01:10:59.100
biased.

01:10:59.100 --> 01:11:01.380
Your text query and
your image query

01:11:01.380 --> 01:11:03.600
aren't really in the
same vector space,

01:11:03.600 --> 01:11:07.780
which means cross-modal
retrieval completely unreliable.

01:11:07.780 --> 01:11:12.760
The Voyage multimodal 3.5 model
uses a single encoder for both.

01:11:12.760 --> 01:11:16.330
So text and visual inputs
go in together and come out

01:11:16.330 --> 01:11:18.650
in one unified vector space.

01:11:18.650 --> 01:11:21.230
This is what really
closes that modality gap.

01:11:21.230 --> 01:11:25.050
So a text query like "smashed
windshield from rear impact,"

01:11:25.050 --> 01:11:28.770
and an image of exactly
that damage are now directly

01:11:28.770 --> 01:11:30.450
comparable one to one.

01:11:30.450 --> 01:11:33.450
It also supports
interleaved text and images.

01:11:33.450 --> 01:11:37.890
So for a PDF page where a table
might sit next to a paragraph,

01:11:37.890 --> 01:11:39.310
they both get captured together.

01:11:39.310 --> 01:11:43.330
Whereas before, they might
get routed separately.

01:11:43.330 --> 01:11:46.330
So now let's go back to
our insurance example

01:11:46.330 --> 01:11:50.130
using an image example
and the actual data flow.

01:11:50.130 --> 01:11:52.470
So a claims adjuster
uploads a photo.

01:11:52.470 --> 01:11:54.950
The query image, which
is a cracked windshield.

01:11:54.950 --> 01:11:58.030
That image goes to the embedder,
which produces a vector.

01:11:58.030 --> 01:12:00.250
And that vector goes
into MongoDB Atlas,

01:12:00.250 --> 01:12:02.890
where it's compared against
your entire vectorized claims

01:12:02.890 --> 01:12:05.130
collection using vector search.

01:12:05.130 --> 01:12:06.630
And what comes back from that?

01:12:06.630 --> 01:12:10.570
The top five most
visually similar

01:12:10.570 --> 01:12:12.327
claims from your history.

01:12:12.327 --> 01:12:14.160
This means that you're
not dependent on just

01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:17.360
keyword matches, but
actual visual similarity.

01:12:17.360 --> 01:12:21.020
And the system found these
because of the damage patterns,

01:12:21.020 --> 01:12:23.360
the angle, the type of impact.

01:12:23.360 --> 01:12:25.720
All of these live
inside of the embedding.

01:12:25.720 --> 01:12:28.640
And because MongoDB stores the
vector in the same document

01:12:28.640 --> 01:12:31.322
as everything else, because
we are a vector database

01:12:31.322 --> 01:12:33.280
and it's storing that
vector next to your claim

01:12:33.280 --> 01:12:35.760
ID, the loss amount, the
adjuster notes, the repair

01:12:35.760 --> 01:12:39.660
history, you get all of that
context back in one query,

01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:43.502
not in a separate system,
but the exact same record.

01:12:43.502 --> 01:12:45.960
So let's zoom out a little bit
and look at the architecture

01:12:45.960 --> 01:12:46.860
in question.

01:12:46.860 --> 01:12:50.560
On the left side, we have what
most RAG stacks look like today.

01:12:50.560 --> 01:12:52.960
You have your operational
database for your application

01:12:52.960 --> 01:12:56.760
data, and a completely separate
vector database for retrieval.

01:12:56.760 --> 01:12:59.360
You have embedding models
running in between.

01:12:59.360 --> 01:13:00.780
A reranker layer.

01:13:00.780 --> 01:13:02.420
So many moving pieces.

01:13:02.420 --> 01:13:04.460
And now let's take a
look at the right side.

01:13:04.460 --> 01:13:06.520
MongoDB Atlas is
a single platform

01:13:06.520 --> 01:13:10.080
with full text search, vector
search, embedding models,

01:13:10.080 --> 01:13:12.770
rerankers, everything
all in one.

01:13:12.770 --> 01:13:15.350
So this means that your
multimodal data, your vectors

01:13:15.350 --> 01:13:16.810
are all in the same document.

01:13:16.810 --> 01:13:19.910
And for those 10,000
insurance claims,

01:13:19.910 --> 01:13:22.370
your vectors actually live
right next to your claim notes

01:13:22.370 --> 01:13:24.870
and your other source data,
which means that there's nothing

01:13:24.870 --> 01:13:28.030
that you need to sync
or glue together.

01:13:28.030 --> 01:13:32.110
And this leaves us with a truly
genuine architectural shift.

01:13:32.110 --> 01:13:34.875
It changes how you handle
your unstructured data, which

01:13:34.875 --> 01:13:36.750
was the old pipeline
that started leaving out

01:13:36.750 --> 01:13:39.390
very crucial information.

01:13:39.390 --> 01:13:41.390
It starts to think
about, well, can

01:13:41.390 --> 01:13:44.770
change how you think about the
modality gap in your retrieval.

01:13:44.770 --> 01:13:48.010
So once again, you have one
encoder, one vector space,

01:13:48.010 --> 01:13:50.750
and your text queries
and your visual documents

01:13:50.750 --> 01:13:52.310
are directly comparable.

01:13:52.310 --> 01:13:55.550
And then it also changes how
you design the database layer

01:13:55.550 --> 01:13:57.810
beneath your AI applications.

01:13:57.810 --> 01:14:00.270
So when your operational
store and your vector store

01:14:00.270 --> 01:14:04.030
are the exact same system, you
eliminate an entire category

01:14:04.030 --> 01:14:06.310
of infrastructure complexity.

01:14:06.310 --> 01:14:08.870
And just to sum it all
up, when your database

01:14:08.870 --> 01:14:10.860
thinks and documents
the same way

01:14:10.860 --> 01:14:13.060
that your models
think in tokens,

01:14:13.060 --> 01:14:15.220
you stop fighting
your infrastructure

01:14:15.220 --> 01:14:17.500
and you start to get results.

01:14:17.500 --> 01:14:20.828
For a deeper dive into vision
RAG and also video RAG,

